  "version": "enrolled",
  "operative_text": "(a_in_person_id) not stated.\n(b_no_id_remedy) not stated.\n(c_mail_ballot_id) not stated.\n(d_proof_of_citizenship)\n(section) R.C. 3503.11(A)(1) (quote ends before \"in conjunction with the person's transaction\")\n(quote) (A)(1) When any person applies for a driver's license, commercial driver's license, a state of Ohio identification card issued under section 4507.50 of the Revised Code, or motorcycle operator's license or endorsement, or the renewal or duplicate of any license or endorsement under Chapter 4506. or 4507. of the Revised Code, and the person presents proof of United States citizenship to the registrar of motor vehicles or the deputy registrar or has previously presented proof of United States citizenship to the registrar or any deputy registrar, the registrar or deputy registrar shall offer the applicant the opportunity to register to vote or to update the applicant's voter registration by electronic means\n(e_other_rule) not stated.\n(other) State transportation budget: appropriations for the 2026-27 biennium and many traffic, road, driver-license, vehicle and transit changes (not read in detail).\n(other) Repeals 4506.072, 4507.021, 4507.063, 4507.511 (enhanced driver license / ID card sections that \"denote citizenship and identity\").\n(other) 4511.093: bans county and township use of traffic-camera enforcement.\n(other) version: 00_IN: no 3503.11 change and no voter-registration text.\n(other) version: 01_RH and 02_PH: 3503.11(A)(1)(b) says the BMV shall not offer voter registration to a person who, per BMV records, is ineligible to register (a records check, no proof of citizenship).\n(other) version: 03_PSC, 04_RS, 05_PS, 06_CH, 07_EN: replace that with the proof-of-citizenship condition on the BMV registration offer (same text in all).\n(other) note: Transportation budget. The only election-law change is R.C. 3503.11(A)(1): the BMV offers online voter registration only to a license or ID-card applicant who presents proof of U.S. citizenship (now or earlier). Everyone who applies at the BMV is covered. The text does not say what counts as proof (the SB 293 definition came later) and does not change paper registration. For the House vote (01_RH), \"true\" means the version had the BMV records-based ineligibility check, not the proof-of-citizenship rule.\n(note) Off this topic: the bill sets no ID or citizenship-proof rule.",
